 [17592186670453 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670453 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $2=\\frac{7}{x+3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670453 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1592a32d-005d-5261-a0e4-09fc6b72ccf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670453 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=-3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{x+3}{7}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition and the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x+3}{7} \\\\ 7\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =7\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x+3}{7}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & =\\cancel{7}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x+3}{\\cancel{7}}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & =x+3 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}-3 & =x+3-3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670454 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670454 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67421" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670454 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $-\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{x-7}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d4080c2-4f5b-58b8-ad8e-0c4cd273e19f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670454 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=7$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{6}{1}=\\frac{x-7}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n-6=x-7\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =x-7 \\\\ -6+7 & =x-7+7 \\\\ 1 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670455 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670455 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670455 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Po}(5),$ compute $P(X=3),$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670455 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "615e1fed-a4a8-59b3-8748-a44e86a3ce62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670455 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Po}(\\lambda ),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Po}(5),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{5}^{x}{e}^{-5}}{x!}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{3}{e}^{-5}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.1404.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670456 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670456 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670456 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x+5}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670456 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c6b4a66-c5e9-5822-b5d3-41ccbed0cc84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670456 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{2}=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{2}=4\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication and the addition principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x+5}{2}\\right) & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x+5}{\\cancel{2}}\\right) & =8 \\\\ x+5 & =8 \\\\ x+5-5 & =8-5 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670457 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670457 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670457 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Po}(2),$ compute $P(X=5),$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670457 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "967d2e95-57ac-5662-bd22-c0decd75429c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670457 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Po}(\\lambda ),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Po}(2),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{2}^{x}{e}^{-2}}{x!}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}{e}^{-2}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.0361.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670458 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670458 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670458 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $-\\frac{5}{3x}=\\frac{5}{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670458 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9691bd1b-5514-58d9-8dcd-30ee08809ee9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670458 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3x}{5}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3x}{5} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ (-5)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x}{-5}\\right) & =(-5)\\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\\\ (\\cancel{-5})\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x}{\\cancel{-5}}\\right) & =(\\cancel{5})\\cdot \\frac{-6}{\\cancel{5}} \\\\ 3x & =-6 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (3x) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (-6) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) & =-\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670459 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670459 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67424" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670459 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation  $\\frac{1}{2x+1}=\\frac{3}{5x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670459 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af124b69-d0c1-5851-8687-aa12571f64c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670459 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+1}{1}=\\frac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $3.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+3}{3}=\\frac{5x}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\frac{6x+3}{3} & =3\\cdot \\frac{5x}{3} \\\\ 6x+3 & =5x \\\\ 6x+3-3 & =5x-3 \\\\ 6x & =5x-3 \\\\ 6x-5x & =5x-3-5x \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670460 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670460 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670460 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $-\\frac{2}{3x}=\\frac{2}{9}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670460 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b88ef01b-7c41-5a6e-8aa7-443a1edbe511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670460 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3x}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3x}{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x}{-2}\\right) & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\cancel{(-2)}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x}{\\cancel{-2}}\\right) & =\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{-9}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ 3x & =-9 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (3x) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(-9) \\\\ \\cancel{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) & =-\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670461 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670461 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{4}{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2a9d9f4-d0de-5d14-970c-e25e6928e45c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186670461 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n